\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces GNN GPU Optimization: Marginal Speedup Contributions. Each bar represents the speedup factor contributed by a specific optimization phase, with labels showing both the speedup multiplier and resulting epoch time. The dominant contribution (12$\times $) comes from batch-parallel processing, validating the hypothesis that sequential processing was the primary bottleneck.}}{95}{figure.caption.561}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Cumulative Speedup Progression (Log Scale). The log-scale y-axis emphasizes the multiplicative nature of optimization phases. Starting from 1$\times $ baseline, optimizations compound to achieve 82.5$\times $ per-epoch speedup on 9-season dataset, reducing epoch time from 33 minutes to 24 seconds.}}{96}{figure.caption.562}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces GPU Utilization: Baseline vs Optimized. Despite processing 9$\times $ more data (2,367 games vs 272 games), the optimized implementation maintains similar GPU utilization (~33\%) with significantly better throughput. The baseline's 45\% utilization on a single season reflects hardware underutilization due to sequential processing bottlenecks.}}{96}{figure.caption.563}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces GNN Training Convergence Through Epoch 49. Validation Brier score (lower is better) showing actual training data from 10 checkpoints (epochs 4, 9, 14, 19, 24, 29, 34, 39, 44, 49) on 9 seasons (2,367 games). The smoothed trend (red line) indicates steady improvement, with early convergence observed by epoch 15, validating that the hierarchical architecture successfully captures game prediction patterns without requiring extensive training.}}{97}{figure.caption.564}%
